WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 1: FDR and the Friendly Torpedo That Almost Killed Him

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:11.120
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories, where we uncover the real, documented, and lesser-known tales behind history's most prominent figures.

2
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.800
[Rebecca] I'm your host, Rebecca.

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.480
[Rebecca] Today we step into the stormy autumn of 1943 with President Franklin Delana Roosevelt.

4
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.040
[Rebecca] We remember FDR for his fireside chats, the New Deal, and steering the Allied powers through World War II.

5
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.280
[Rebecca] But history often turns on absurd, razor-thin margins.

6
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:42.560
[Rebecca] In November of 1943, on a voyage shrouded in total wartime secrecy, an American warship accidentally fired a live, fully armed torpedo directly at the battleship carrying the Commander-in-Chief.

7
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.840
[Rebecca] And now, here is George with the story.

8
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:59.280
[George] In the second week of November 1943, a thick, cold fog blanketed the waters off Hampton Roads, Virginia.

9
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:10.880
[George] Sliding quietly out into the Atlantic under the cloak of night was the mightiest warship in the United States Navy, the newly commissioned battleship USS Iowa.

10
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:19.600
[George] She was an 88,000 horsepower fortress of steel, carrying nine 16-inch guns and armor plates over a foot thick.

11
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.040
[George] Aboard was a passenger whose presence was the most closely guarded secret of the war.

12
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.120
[George] President Franklin Delano Roosevelt, accompanied by Army Chief of Staff George C.

13
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:39.680
[George] Marshall, Admiral Ernest King, and his closest diplomatic advisors, Roosevelt was embarking on Operation Sextant.

14
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.440
[George] Crossing the Atlantic to meet Winston Churchill in Cairo and Joseph Stalin in Tehran.

15
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:55.120
[George] It was to be the first face-to-face summit of the big three leaders, a meeting that would decide the timing of D-Day and the post-war partition of Europe.

16
00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:05.400
[George] For security against German U-boat Wolfpacks that infested the Atlantic sea lanes, the Iowa was surrounded by a protective screen of cruisers and destroyers.

17
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.760
[George] Among these escorts was a fresh Fletcher class destroyer, Hull No.

18
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.760
[George] 579, the USS William D.

19
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.360
[George] Porter, commanded by Lieutenant Commander Wilfred Walter.

20
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.800
[George] To her crew of mostly green recruits, the porter was their ticket to glory.

21
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:32.280
[George] But from the moment she pulled out of Norfolk, the ship seemed cursed with astonishing misfortune.

22
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:45.560
[George] While maneuvering away from the dock, the porter's stern anchor had hooked onto the railing of a neighboring sister destroyer, ripping away stanchions, railings, and a lifeboat before freeing herself.

23
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:53.320
[George] Captain Walter had delivered a stern dressing down to his young crew, hoping that humiliating mishap would be their last.

24
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.560
[George] It was only the prelude.

25
00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.760
[George] Twenty-four hours into the voyage, the convoy ran into rough seas.

26
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:07.160
[George] The wind whipped up towering whitecaps, and the destroyers rolled violently in the swells.

27
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.880
[George] Suddenly, a massive underwater explosion shook the ocean.

28
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.440
[George] A thunderous shockwave hammered against the hull of the Iowa.

29
00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.960
[George] Aboard the battleship sirens wailed.

30
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:27.640
[George] General quarters, gun crews sprinted to their battle stations, lookout binoculars swept the horizons.

31
00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:34.920
[George] The Iowa's massive turbine surged to life, healing the ship into violent zigzag maneuvers.

32
00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.840
[George] German U-boats were presumed to be attacking the president's flagship.

33
00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:46.240
[George] Depth charge crews stood ready on all destroyers, but there were no German periscopes in sight.

34
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:51.280
[George] Minutes later, a shamefaced signal flashed across the water from the William D.

35
00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:51.920
[George] Porter.

36
00:03:52.160 --> 00:04:00.160
[George] An improperly secured depth charge had rolled off her stern rack during a heavy roll and detonated upon reaching its pressure depth.

37
00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.600
[George] Admiral Ernest King was apoplectic.

38
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:13.600
[George] To broadcast an accidental depth charge explosion in the middle of the Atlantic was like lighting a flare for every Nazi submarine within a hundred miles.

39
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:20.480
[George] Captain Walter was reprimanded via blinker light, and the men of the Porter hung their heads in disgrace.

40
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.960
[George] They desperately needed an opportunity to prove their competence.

41
00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:21.160
[George] FDR smiled broadly, puffing his cigarette and applauding the gunnery.

42
00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:25.560
[George] Across six thousand yards of open water, Captain Walter of the William D.

43
00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.480
[George] Porter watched the demonstration.

44
00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:32.920
[George] Here was his chance to redeem his ship in the eyes of the President and the High Command.

45
00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:41.400
[George] Walter ordered his crew to conduct a simulated torpedo drill, using the distant colossal silhouette of the Iowa as their mock target.

46
00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:47.640
[George] On the Porter's deck, the torpedo mount was manned by torpedo men third class Lawton Dawson.

47
00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:54.760
[George] In a live fire exercise, the Mark 15 torpedoes carried 1,800 pounds of high explosives.

48
00:05:54.760 --> 00:06:05.480
[George] For a dry drill, Navy regulations strictly mandated that the small explosive primer charges used to expel the torpedoes from their tubes must be removed.

49
00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:09.000
[George] Dawson removed the primers from tubes one and three.

50
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:16.520
[George] But on tube number two, in the excitement and noise of the exercise, he made a catastrophic oversight.

51
00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.600
[George] He left the primer in place.

52
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.920
[George] On the bridge, Captain Walter shouted the standard drill command.

53
00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:26.040
[George] Mount two, stand by, fire two.

54
00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:31.720
[George] Instead of a hollow metallic click, a terrifying whoosh echoed across the deck.

55
00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:36.280
[George] An exhaust plume of white compressed gas billowed into the air.

56
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:52.680
[George] Before the horrified eyes of the crew, a two-ton, fully armed, live torpedo splashed into the Atlantic, its propellers screaming to life at forty-five knots, churning a deadly white, frothy wake, heading straight for the Iowa.

57
00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.800
[George] Utter chaos engulfed the bridge of the William D.

58
00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:56.440
[George] Porter.

59
00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:00.920
[George] Captain Walter stared in disbelief, turning pale as chalk.

60
00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:06.120
[George] A live torpedo was speeding directly toward the President of the United States.

61
00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.160
[George] They had less than two minutes before impact.

62
00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:14.840
[George] Strict radio silence was in effect to conceal the mission from Axis listening stations.

63
00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:24.400
[George] Desperate to warn the Iowa without breaking radio protocols, Walter ordered his signalman to flash an urgent warning with the Aldis lamp.

64
00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:32.560
[George] The signalman, shaking with adrenaline, aimed the lamp at the Iowa's towering bridge and began frantically clacking the shutter.

65
00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.640
[George] Torpedo in water.

66
00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:37.760
[George] Steer right, he intended to say.

67
00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:43.360
[George] Instead, in his blinding panic, the signalman transposed letters.

68
00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:49.680
[George] What the Iowa's lookouts read was Porter is backing up full speed.

69
00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.080
[George] Aboard the Iowa, the bridge crew blinked in irritation.

70
00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.680
[George] They flashed back.

71
00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.000
[George] Porter, clarify message.

72
00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:06.880
[George] The torpedo was closing at 45 knots, cutting through the water like a predatory shark just 4,000 yards away.

73
00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:13.520
[George] Seeing that visual signals were failing, Captain Walter threw naval regulations out the window.

74
00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:21.440
[George] He grabbed the TBS radio microphone, broke radio silence, and screamed over the fleet's open frequency.

75
00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:26.720
[George] Lion, look right!

76
00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.480
[George] Torpedo in the water!

77
00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.920
[George] Lion was the Iowa's classified code name!

78
00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.160
[George] The voice on the Iowa's radio demanded authentication.

79
00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:38.720
[George] Walter shouted back, no time!

80
00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:40.000
[George] Turn hard right!

81
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.560
[George] Torpedo in water to starboard!

82
00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:48.480
[George] Suddenly, lookouts on the Iowa spotted the churning white line approaching the ship's flank.

83
00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.880
[George] General Quarter's sirens screamed.

84
00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.680
[George] The helmsman spun the wheel hard left.

85
00:08:53.680 --> 00:09:04.440
[George] The Iowa's four giant screws churned the ocean into a white cauldron as forty-five thousand tons of armor heeled violently into a desperate turn.

86
00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:10.280
[George] Inside his state room, President Roosevelt felt the violent lurch of the deck.

87
00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:16.120
[George] Secret Service agent Mike Riley rushed into the room, shouting that a torpedo was inbound.

88
00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:23.880
[George] Most leaders would have ducked for cover inside the Conning Tower, but Roosevelt's eyes lit up with intense curiosity.

89
00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.120
[George] Wheel me to the railing, Mike, Roosevelt insisted.

90
00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.200
[George] I want to see it.

91
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:34.520
[George] Riley wheeled the president out onto the deck to the starboard railing.

92
00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:46.600
[George] With his fedora firmly on his head, Franklin Roosevelt watched the foaming silver track of the torpedo race across the Atlantic directly toward his ship.

93
00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:52.520
[George] For sixty agonizing seconds, every officer aboard the Iowa held their breath.

94
00:09:52.520 --> 00:10:04.680
[George] If the torpedo struck amidships near the ammunition magazines, the commander-in-chief, the joint chiefs of staff, and the entire leadership of the American war effort would be incinerated.

95
00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:08.840
[George] The Iowa's engines groaned under maximum emergency power.

96
00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:18.760
[George] Gun crews on the secondary batteries began wildly firing 20 millimeter shells into the ocean, attempting to detonate the torpedo before it reached the hull.

97
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:24.120
[George] At the very last moment, the Iowa's sharp evasive turn paid off.

98
00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:33.720
[George] The torpedo sliced through the water, missing the stern by roughly 3,000 yards, passing through the turbulent churn of the battleship's wake.

99
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.840
[George] Moments later, the torpedo detonated.

100
00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:42.920
[George] A magnificent geyser of white foam and salt water erupted hundreds of feet into the sky.

101
00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:49.600
[George] The concussion shook the deck of the Iowa, spraying fine ocean mist onto Roosevelt's face.

102
00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:56.240
[George] The president let out a low chuckle, removed his cigarette holder, and turned to his aides with a grin.

103
00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:00.400
[George] Aboard the Iowa's bridge, however, nobody was smiling.

104
00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:07.200
[George] Admiral King and Captain McCrea were utterly convinced they had just survived an assassination plot.

105
00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.080
[George] Suspicion immediately fell upon the William D.

106
00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:10.800
[George] Porter.

107
00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:24.080
[George] The Iowa swiveled all nine of her massive 16-inch turrets, guns capable of firing 2700-pound armor-piercing shells twenty miles, and locked them squarely onto the diminutive destroyer.

108
00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:32.720
[George] The porter was ordered to stand down, surrender its logs, and report directly to naval authorities in Bermuda under armed escort.

109
00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:36.480
[George] Upon arriving in Bermuda, Marines boarded the William D.

110
00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:43.120
[George] Porter and placed the entire crew, officers, sailors, and cooks alike, under military arrest.

111
00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.720
[George] Interrogations were fierce.

112
00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:58.800
[George] Naval intelligence combed through every crewman's background, searching for German saboteurs.

113
00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:11.200
[George] Finally, Lawton Dawson, weeping in remorse, confessed that he had inadvertently left the primer in tube number two, and in his panic had thrown the spent primer casing overboard to hide his mistake.

114
00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:16.240
[George] Dawson was court-martialed and sentenced to 14 years of hard labor.

115
00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:21.600
[George] But when the verdict reached the Oval Office, President Roosevelt intervened personally.

116
00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:33.720
[George] Writing to the Secretary of the Navy, FDR stated that no harm had been done, that it was clearly an accident born of training frenzy, and asked that Dawson be pardoned and restored to duty.

117
00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:38.120
[George] The Navy complied, but they never quite forgave the porter.

118
00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:44.120
[George] The destroyer was exiled to the frozen fog-choked outpost of the Aleutian Islands.

119
00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:47.160
[George] For the remainder of the war, whenever the William D.

120
00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:55.240
[George] Porter pulled into a naval anchorage, other American ships would hail her over the radio with the same sardonic joke: Don't shoot, boys!

121
00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.240
[George] We're Republicans.

122
00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.000
[George] The saga of the William D.

123
00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:03.480
[Rebecca] Porter remains one of the most astonishing near misses in modern history.

124
00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:19.480
[Rebecca] Had that torpedo struck the Iowa's magazine, the course of World War II, the timing of the cross-channel invasion, the atomic bomb program, and the entire architecture of the post-war world would have been radically altered in a single blinding flash.

125
00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:30.600
[Rebecca] Roosevelt's unflappable comm at the railing, asking to be wheeled closer to watch the weapon approach, captures the indomitable spirit of a leader who refused to let fear dictate his life.

126
00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.640
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

127
00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:34.920
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

128
00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:39.720
[Rebecca] Join us next time as we unearth another forgotten chapter from the pages of history.

129
00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.360
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

